Section 2(1)(a) specifically states that an industrial dispute includes:

* A dispute as to the terms and conditions of employment;
* A dispute as to the wages, salaries, or other remuneration of employees;
* A dispute as to the hours of work, rest periods, or holidays;
* A dispute as to the working conditions, safety, health, or welfare of employees;
* A dispute as to the appointment, promotion, transfer, demotion, dismissal, or termination of employment;
* A dispute as to the payment of wages, salaries, or other remuneration in advance or arrears;
* A dispute as to the payment of leave, holiday, or other benefits;
* A dispute as to the working hours, overtime, or rest periods; and
* Any other matter that may lead to a lockout, strike, or other form of industrial action.

Section 2(1)(b) also states that an industrial dispute includes any act or omission by an employer or employee that leads to a lockout, strike, or other form of industrial action.

It's worth noting that the definition of industrial dispute is broad and encompasses various aspects of employment relations.

